\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Gumbel Soft Thinking{} feeds the sampled token plus a residual. At each thinking step the model gives next-token probabilities. Sampling feeds one token. Gumbel Soft Thinking{} adds Gumbel noise and feeds the softmax-weighted mix of token embeddings. By the Gumbel-max trick, its top token is distributed exactly as the sampled one, and it is the same token when both use the same noise in the same context, as in our coupled ablation (\cref  {sec:q4}). So all it adds is the residual (blue). The random-residual control{} keeps the top token and the residual's size but moves the residual to a random token (orange). Weights are illustrative.\relax }}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:concept}{{1}{2}{\GST {} feeds the sampled token plus a residual. At each thinking step the model gives next-token probabilities. Sampling feeds one token. \GST {} adds Gumbel noise and feeds the softmax-weighted mix of token embeddings. By the Gumbel-max trick, its top token is distributed exactly as the sampled one, and it is the same token when both use the same noise in the same context, as in our coupled ablation (\cref {sec:q4}). So all it adds is the residual (blue). The \ctrl {} keeps the top token and the residual's size but moves the residual to a random token (orange). Weights are illustrative.\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:concept@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][2][]2}}
