\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The runner-up is stored and can be read back, but stays dormant on the model's own path. At forks where the model followed the chosen token, we force a 64-token continuation into the cache built from the 50/50 blend and into a control cache (chosen token blended 50/50 with the third-ranked candidate), and score the next-token predictions from 0 (as if the runner-up had been written) to 1 (as if the chosen token had). Readback = control minus blend; above 0 means the runner-up is read back from the cache. (a) Per fork: with the runner-up's text, +0.44 (number vs number), +0.11 (number vs word) and +0.33 (word vs word); with the model's own text, $-0.01$ to $+0.06$, with every CI including 0. (b) With the runner-up's text, the readback stays above 0 in every position bin up to 64 tokens for number vs number and word vs word. (c) With the model's own text, the CI includes 0 in every bin for number vs number and word vs word. Positions are binned on a log scale; \cref  {fig:readback-unbinned} shows them unbinned. Bars and bands: 95\% bootstrap CIs over problems. $n$ = 96, 34 and 139 forks. Exact 50/50 blends are the most favourable case for the runner-up, so these values are likely an upper bound for real forks, if readback grows with the residual (\cref  {sec:q3}).\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:readback}{{4}{6}{The runner-up is stored and can be read back, but stays dormant on the model's own path. At forks where the model followed the chosen token, we force a 64-token continuation into the cache built from the 50/50 blend and into a control cache (chosen token blended 50/50 with the third-ranked candidate), and score the next-token predictions from 0 (as if the runner-up had been written) to 1 (as if the chosen token had). Readback = control minus blend; above 0 means the runner-up is read back from the cache. (a) Per fork: with the runner-up's text, +0.44 (number vs number), +0.11 (number vs word) and +0.33 (word vs word); with the model's own text, $-0.01$ to $+0.06$, with every CI including 0. (b) With the runner-up's text, the readback stays above 0 in every position bin up to 64 tokens for number vs number and word vs word. (c) With the model's own text, the CI includes 0 in every bin for number vs number and word vs word. Positions are binned on a log scale; \cref {fig:readback-unbinned} shows them unbinned. Bars and bands: 95\% bootstrap CIs over problems. $n$ = 96, 34 and 139 forks. Exact 50/50 blends are the most favourable case for the runner-up, so these values are likely an upper bound for real forks, if readback grows with the residual (\cref {sec:q3}).\relax }{figure.caption.18}{}}
\newlabel{fig:readback@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][6][]6}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Answer.}{6}{section*.16}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}Q2: Is the runner-up stored, and can later text read it back?}{6}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:q2}{{4.2}{6}{Q2: Is the runner-up stored, and can later text read it back?}{subsection.4.2}{}}
\newlabel{sec:q2@cref}{{[subsection][2][4]4.2}{[1][6][]6}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Readback.}{6}{section*.17}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The runner-up is stored mostly in the early layers. At each fork (50/50 blend; forks where the model followed the chosen token) we swap the keys and values of the fork position between the blend cache and the control cache at a set of layers, and report the pooled share of the blend--control readback gap this closes (summed over forks). Remove (filled): put control K,V into the blend cache (are these layers needed?). Insert (open): put blend K,V into the control cache (are they enough?). (a, b) Layers 0--7 close 0.77 / 0.71 (number vs number, remove / insert) and 0.77 / 0.91 (word vs word) of the gap; layers 12--27 close 0.12 / 0.26 and 0.08 / 0.14. (c) Number vs number, single layers: the early effect sits in layers 0--2 (remove 0.38, 0.21, 0.20). Outside layers 0--3, only layers 7 (0.13 / 0.12) and 13 (0.08 / 0.13) stand out, with CIs excluding 0; every other layer closes at most 0.05. Note the different $y$-axis in (c). The single-layer analysis was not pre-registered; \cref  {tab:fig5-singles} shows these layers survive a Bonferroni correction over all 56 single-layer tests. \Cref  {tab:fig5-robust} shows the shares hold when any one problem, or forks with a small gap, are dropped; \cref  {fig:perfork} shows the per-fork spread, which is wide for layer 0. Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CIs over problems. 96 forks from 35 problems (number vs number) and 139 forks from 40 problems (word vs word). Number vs word is not shown: its gap ($+0.11$) is too small to localise (\cref  {tab:fig5-nw}). All shares are measured at exact 50/50 blends, the most favourable case for the runner-up, so these shares are likely an upper bound for real forks.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.20}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:patch}{{5}{7}{The runner-up is stored mostly in the early layers. At each fork (50/50 blend; forks where the model followed the chosen token) we swap the keys and values of the fork position between the blend cache and the control cache at a set of layers, and report the pooled share of the blend--control readback gap this closes (summed over forks). Remove (filled): put control K,V into the blend cache (are these layers needed?). Insert (open): put blend K,V into the control cache (are they enough?). (a, b) Layers 0--7 close 0.77 / 0.71 (number vs number, remove / insert) and 0.77 / 0.91 (word vs word) of the gap; layers 12--27 close 0.12 / 0.26 and 0.08 / 0.14. (c) Number vs number, single layers: the early effect sits in layers 0--2 (remove 0.38, 0.21, 0.20). Outside layers 0--3, only layers 7 (0.13 / 0.12) and 13 (0.08 / 0.13) stand out, with CIs excluding 0; every other layer closes at most 0.05. Note the different $y$-axis in (c). The single-layer analysis was not pre-registered; \cref {tab:fig5-singles} shows these layers survive a Bonferroni correction over all 56 single-layer tests. \Cref {tab:fig5-robust} shows the shares hold when any one problem, or forks with a small gap, are dropped; \cref {fig:perfork} shows the per-fork spread, which is wide for layer 0. Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CIs over problems. 96 forks from 35 problems (number vs number) and 139 forks from 40 problems (word vs word). Number vs word is not shown: its gap ($+0.11$) is too small to localise (\cref {tab:fig5-nw}). All shares are measured at exact 50/50 blends, the most favourable case for the runner-up, so these shares are likely an upper bound for real forks.\relax }{figure.caption.20}{}}
\newlabel{fig:patch@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][7][]7}}
