\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{The top token of Gumbel Soft Thinking{} is a sampled token.}{8}{section*.22}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{The residual is real but small.}{8}{section*.24}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Noise turns real forks into lopsided blends, and Gumbel's winner is a sample. A fork is a thinking step where the pre-noise runner-up holds at least 25\% of the weight (9.2\% of steps for vanilla, 13.0\% for Gumbel, 13.3\% for Dirichlet). (a) Share of forks by the top weight of the blend actually fed. Vanilla feeds the pre-noise weights unchanged, so it shows what the model wanted: every fork is balanced (top weight at most 0.75, by the fork rule). Gumbel feeds a near one-token blend (top weight at least 0.9) at 47\% of forks and Dirichlet at 37\%. The median fed blend has a residual (weight off the top token) of 0.12 for Gumbel and 0.17 for Dirichlet, against 0.44 before noise. (b) How often noise makes a token other than the pre-noise top one the winner, minus how often ordinary sampling would (1 minus the pre-noise top weight), with forks binned by the sampling rate. Gumbel matches sampling: 0.00 [$-0.01$, 0.02] overall and within 0.04 in every bin with at least 100 forks. Dirichlet flips less often than sampling in every bin, $-0.03$ [$-0.04$, $-0.02$] overall (dashed line). Vanilla never flips (not shown). Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CIs over problems. 3,774 (vanilla), 5,761 (Gumbel) and 4,978 (Dirichlet) forks from 20 GSM8K problems; each arm is its own run, so the forks differ.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fed}{{6}{9}{Noise turns real forks into lopsided blends, and Gumbel's winner is a sample. A fork is a thinking step where the pre-noise runner-up holds at least 25\% of the weight (9.2\% of steps for vanilla, 13.0\% for Gumbel, 13.3\% for Dirichlet). (a) Share of forks by the top weight of the blend actually fed. Vanilla feeds the pre-noise weights unchanged, so it shows what the model wanted: every fork is balanced (top weight at most 0.75, by the fork rule). Gumbel feeds a near one-token blend (top weight at least 0.9) at 47\% of forks and Dirichlet at 37\%. The median fed blend has a residual (weight off the top token) of 0.12 for Gumbel and 0.17 for Dirichlet, against 0.44 before noise. (b) How often noise makes a token other than the pre-noise top one the winner, minus how often ordinary sampling would (1 minus the pre-noise top weight), with forks binned by the sampling rate. Gumbel matches sampling: 0.00 [$-0.01$, 0.02] overall and within 0.04 in every bin with at least 100 forks. Dirichlet flips less often than sampling in every bin, $-0.03$ [$-0.04$, $-0.02$] overall (dashed line). Vanilla never flips (not shown). Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CIs over problems. 3,774 (vanilla), 5,761 (Gumbel) and 4,978 (Dirichlet) forks from 20 GSM8K problems; each arm is its own run, so the forks differ.\relax }{figure.caption.23}{}}
\newlabel{fig:fed@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][8][]9}}
